modules.practiceExercise:
Set up a basic React application and observe HMR in action.

1.  Initialize a new React project using Create React App: npx create-react-app hmr-demo
2.  Navigate into the project directory: cd hmr-demo
3.  Start the development server: npm start (or yarn start)
4.  Open src/App.js. Modify the text inside the header (e.g., change 'Edit src/App.js and save to reload.' to 'HMR is awesome!'). Observe that the change appears instantly in your browser without a full page refresh.
5.  Add a simple counter component: Create a new file src/Counter.js:

    import React, { useState } from 'react';

    function Counter() {
      const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
      return (
        <div>
          <p>Count: {count}</p>
          <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>
        </div>
      );
    }

    export default Counter;

6.  Import and render Counter in App.js:

    import Counter from './Counter';

    function App() {
      return (
        <div className="App">
          {/* ... existing content ... */}
          <Counter />
        </div>
      );
    }

7.  Interact with the counter to increment its value several times. While the counter shows a non-zero value, modify the text within the <p> tag in src/Counter.js (e.g., change 'Count:' to 'Current Count:').

Observe: The text updates, but the count value remains unchanged. This demonstrates HMR preserving the component's state during a hot update. This hands-on exercise helps you directly experience and understand the core benefit of HMR.
